;;; Set.mon --- Finite extensional sets as the free finite-powerset algebra

:author   Laluxx
:version  3.0.0
:keywords set finite powerset extensional quotient semilattice lattice boolean-algebra monad mathematics unicode

;;; Commentary:

 A Set is not a sequence with uniqueness bolted on.
 It is an extensional mathematical object.

 This module therefore specifies finite sets by their laws and observations,
 not by a privileged machine representation.  The compiler may erase this
 implementation into a hash set, tree, HAMT, bitset, perfect finite-domain
 encoding, or a representation specialized from whole-program knowledge.  It
 may not change the observable theory stated here.

 Mathematically:

   Set a  =  P_f(a)

 the finite powerset of a.

 Equivalently, Set a is the free finite join-semilattice with bottom generated
 by a.  Its unit inserts a generator, its join is union, and its monadic
 multiplication is union of a finite family:

   eta_a : a -> P_f(a)
   eta_a(x) = {x}

   mu_a : P_f(P_f(a)) -> P_f(a)
   mu_a(F) = union F

 Hence the familiar finite-powerset monad:

   pure x      = {x}
   xs >>= f    = union { f x | x in xs }

 This is finite nondeterminism as ordinary mathematics, not a runtime effect.


 EXTENSIONALITY
 ──────────────

 The source implementation observes the compiler's canonical finite-set
 representation through one private eliminator.  The resulting list is *not
 the set*.  It is merely a representative of an extensional quotient.
 Presentations are identified by membership:

   xs ~= =ys    iff    ∀x. x in xs <=> x in ys

 so, in particular:

   from-list [1 1 2]  ==  from-list [2 1]  ==  {1 2}

 Order and multiplicity are semantically nonexistent.

 This has an important consequence for API design: no exported function may
 reveal presentation order or multiplicity.  In particular there is no
 unconstrained

   to-list :: {a} -> [a]
   choose  :: {a} -> Maybe a

 because neither is a function on extensional sets.  A canonical sequence can
 be observed only when the element type supplies enough structure to choose a
 canonical ordering:

   to-ascending-list :: (Eq a, Ord a) => {a} -> [a]

 Likewise, no Foldable or Traversable instance is provided.  A generic fold
 can distinguish both permutation and duplication, so it would descend from
 presentations rather than from the quotient.  If core later gains an
 explicit commutative-idempotent-monoid / join-semilattice abstraction, Set's
 canonical eliminator should be the corresponding homomorphic fold.


 EQUALITY IS AN OBSERVATION, NOT A CONSTRUCTION REQUIREMENT
 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

 Construction and positive algebra do not need Eq:

   singleton         :: a -> {a}
   from-list         :: [a] -> {a}
   insert            :: a -> {a} -> {a}
   union             :: {a} -> {a} -> {a}
   unions            :: {{a}} -> {a}
   image             :: (a -> b) -> {a} -> {b}
   cartesian-product :: {a} -> {b} -> {(a,b)}
   power-set         :: {a} -> {{a}}
   bind-set          :: {a} -> (a -> {b}) -> {b}

 Duplicate representatives are harmless because the quotient removes them.
 This is precisely what lets Set be an ordinary Functor and Monad without an
 illegal Eq constraint appearing in map or bind.

 Eq appears exactly where an operation must *decide* extensional identity:
 membership, cardinality, intersection, difference, subset, deletion, and
 canonical enumeration.


 FINITENESS AND UNIVERSES
 ────────────────────────

 Every value of type {a} is finite, even when a itself is infinite.
 Consequently there is always bottom:

   empty-set = empty set

 but in general there is no top element.  For example, {Int} cannot contain
 every Int while remaining a finite value.

 Thus finite subsets of an arbitrary a form a distributive lattice with
 bottom and relative difference -- more precisely, a generalized Boolean
 algebra -- not necessarily a bounded Boolean algebra.

 When a type is genuinely finite, the Finite class supplies its universe:

   universe :: Finite a => {a}

 and {a} then becomes the full powerset Boolean algebra P(a).  Absolute
 complement and the empty-family intersection become total only under this
 stronger hypothesis.


 SURFACE MATHEMATICS
 ───────────────────

 Type syntax:

   Set       :: Type -> Type
   {a}       == Set a
   {Int}     == Set Int
   {{Int}}   == Set (Set Int)

 Value syntax:

   {}          ==> empty-set
   {x}         ==> singleton x
   {x y z}     ==> from-list (list x y z)

 Bounded comprehension:

   {x in xs | p x}     ==> filter-set p xs

 or, for an image-producing comprehension, equivalently via the powerset
 monad:

   {f x | x in xs}     ==> image f xs

 Unbounded predicates such as

   {x in Int | x > 0}

 belong to refinement/proposition syntax, not to this finite value type.

 The intended mathematical operators are:

   x in A        membership       x ∈ A
   A union B     join             A ∪ B
   A intersect B meet             A ∩ B
   A minus B     difference       A ∖ B
   A xor B       symmetric diff   A △ B
   A subset B    inclusion        A ⊆ B
   A proper B    strict inclusion A ⊂ B
   A times B     product          A × B
   powerset A    finite powerset  ℘ A
   union F       family join      ⋃ F


 COMPILER CONTRACT
 ─────────────────

 The compiler should know the *shape* of set syntax and may know optimization
 identities, but it should not own set algorithms.  The semantic lowering
 boundary is this module:

   {}        -> empty-set
   {x}       -> singleton x
   {x y z}   -> from-list (...)
   {a} type  -> Set a

 Optimizations are proofs of observational equivalence to these definitions.
 The core implementation is therefore an executable specification for every
 optimized backend representation.

;;; Code:

import Data.Bool
import Data.Eq
import Data.Either
import Data.Maybe
import Data.Ord
import Data.Tuple
import Data.Functor
import Data.Semigroup
import Control.Applicative
import Control.Monad
import Sequence
import Test.QuickCheck

module Data.Set where
  :type
    Set

  :finite-universe
    Finite universe complement

  :membership
    Membership membership? member? in-set? ∈ contains? ∋ not-member? ∉

  :construction
    empty-set ∅ singleton from-list

  :observation
    cardinality size null? non-empty? singleton?
    to-ascending-list elements

  :update
    insert delete remove

  :join-algebra
    union U ∪ unions ⋃

  :meet-and-difference
    intersection ∩
    intersections ⋂ intersections-in intersections-all
    difference ∖ relative-complement complement-in
    symmetric-difference △

  :inclusion
    subset? ⊆ not-subset? ⊈
    proper-subset? ⊂ not-proper-subset? ⊄
    superset? ⊇ not-superset? ⊉
    proper-superset? ⊃ not-proper-superset? ⊅
    disjoint? overlaps?

  :selection
    filter-set partition-set exists? forall?

  :functorial
    image preimage-on

  :products
    cartesian-product × disjoint-union ⊎

  :powerset
    power-set ℘

  :ordered
    minimum maximum

  :monad
    bind-set

  :laws
    SetLaws FiniteSetLaws


;;; Mathematical reader surface

type-syntax {_} Set

| Cardinality is an observation, so |_| lowers to cardinality.  Binary set
| operators are ordinary exported aliases and should receive their usual
| mathematical precedence from the reader/operator table.
reader-syntax Int
  |$| cardinality


;;; Representation

| Set has one representation: the compiler's canonical finite-set value.
| This private eliminator exposes a finite presentation to the executable
| specification below.  Public functions remain ordinary module functions;
| the compiler never dispatches on names such as union or intersection.
|
| The current declaration language has no abstract type declaration yet, so
| this private constructor registers the nominal type constructor only.  No
| Data.Set definition constructs or eliminates it; it is not the value ABI.
data Set a
  = SetValue [a]


define set-presentation :: {a} -> [a]
  xs -> __rt_set_elements xs


;;; Private presentation kernel

| Nothing in this section is part of the public theory.  Each helper is valid
| only because every exported use of it is invariant under permutation and
| duplication of the private presentation.


define set-list-member? :: Eq a => a -> [a] -> Bool
  _ []     -> False
  x [y|ys] -> if eq? x y then True else set-list-member? x ys


define set-list-append :: [a] -> [a] -> [a]
  [] ys     -> ys
  [x|xs] ys -> prepend x (set-list-append xs ys)


define set-list-map :: (a -> b) -> [a] -> [b]
  _ []     -> []
  f [x|xs] -> prepend (f x) (set-list-map f xs)


define set-list-filter :: (a -> Bool) -> [a] -> [a]
  _ [] -> []
  p [x|xs] ->
    if p x
      then prepend x (set-list-filter p xs)
      else set-list-filter p xs


define set-list-foldl :: (b -> a -> b) -> b -> [a] -> b
  _ acc []     -> acc
  f acc [x|xs] -> set-list-foldl f (f acc x) xs


define set-list-any? :: [a] -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  [] _     -> False
  [x|xs] p -> if p x then True else set-list-any? xs p


define set-list-all? :: [a] -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  [] _     -> True
  [x|xs] p -> if p x then set-list-all? xs p else False


define set-list-reverse-into :: [a] -> [a] -> [a]
  acc []     -> acc
  acc [x|xs] -> set-list-reverse-into (prepend x acc) xs


define set-list-reverse :: [a] -> [a]
  xs -> set-list-reverse-into [] xs


| Extensional deduplication for observations that need equality.  The output
| order is deliberately private and must never escape as a public list.
define set-list-unique-into :: Eq a => [a] -> [a] -> [a]
  seen [] -> set-list-reverse seen
  seen [x|xs] ->
    if set-list-member? x seen
      then set-list-unique-into seen xs
      else set-list-unique-into (prepend x seen) xs


define set-list-unique :: Eq a => [a] -> [a]
  xs -> set-list-unique-into [] xs


define set-list-length :: [a] -> Int
  []     -> 0
  [x|xs] -> 1 + set-list-length xs


define set-list-delete :: Eq a => a -> [a] -> [a]
  _ [] -> []
  x [y|ys] ->
    if eq? x y
      then set-list-delete x ys
      else prepend y (set-list-delete x ys)


| Canonicalization for the one public sequence observation.  Requiring both
| Eq and Ord states the real obligation: equality removes multiplicity and
| ordering chooses a presentation.  A lawful Ord should be coherent with Eq.
define set-list-insert-ascending :: (Eq a, Ord a) => a -> [a] -> [a]
  x [] -> list x
  x [y|ys] -> if eq? x y then prepend y ys else if lt? x y then prepend x (prepend y ys) else prepend y (set-list-insert-ascending x ys)


define set-list-canonical :: (Eq a, Ord a) => [a] -> [a]
  []     -> []
  [x|xs] -> set-list-insert-ascending x (set-list-canonical xs)


;;; Construction


define [empty-set :: {a}] (set)
define [∅ :: {a}] empty-set


define singleton :: a -> {a}
  x -> set x


| from-list is the quotient map from finite presentations to finite sets.
| It intentionally does not deduplicate: quotient semantics, not constructor
| normalization, establish uniqueness.
define from-list :: [a] -> {a}
  xs -> set xs


;;; Membership

| Membership is parameterized by a unary collection constructor so the member
| type is tied to the collection's parameter.  Set's instance asks for Eq
| exactly because membership is an extensional equality observation.
class Membership s where
  membership? :: Eq a => a -> s a -> Bool

  ;; s :: Type -> Type


define set-member? :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> Bool
  x values -> set-list-member? x (set-presentation values)


define member? :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> Bool
  x xs -> set-member? x xs


instance Membership Set
  membership? x xs -> set-member? x xs


define in-set? ∈ :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> Bool
  x xs -> set-member? x xs


define contains? ∋ :: Eq a => {a} -> a -> Bool
  xs x -> set-member? x xs


define not-member? ∉ :: (Membership s, Eq a) => a -> s a -> Bool
  x xs -> not? (membership? x xs)


;;; Extensional observations


define null? :: {a} -> Bool
  values -> list-empty? (set-presentation values)


define non-empty? :: {a} -> Bool
  xs -> not? (null? xs)


| Cardinality is the number of equivalence classes represented by the private
| presentation, hence Eq is genuinely required by this reference model.
define cardinality :: Eq a => {a} -> Int
  values -> set-list-length (set-list-unique (set-presentation values))


define singleton? :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
  xs -> cardinality xs = 1


| This is deliberately the ONLY generic escape from Set to an ordered
| sequence.  Unlike a presentation-revealing to-list, it is a genuine function
| of the extensional set whenever Eq and Ord are coherent.
define to-ascending-list :: (Eq a, Ord a) => {a} -> [a]
  :alias elements
  values -> set-list-canonical (set-presentation values)


;;; Elementary updates

| insert needs no Eq: adding another representative of an existing element
| does not change the extensional set.
define insert :: a -> {a} -> {a}
  x values -> from-list (prepend x (set-presentation values))


| delete removes every equal representative so the result is independent of
| presentation multiplicity.
define delete :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> {a}
  x values -> from-list (set-list-delete x (set-presentation values))


define remove :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> {a}
  x xs -> delete x xs


;;; Join-semilattice structure

| Union is literally concatenation at the presentation level.  Associativity,
| commutativity, idempotence, and the empty identity arise extensionally.
define union ∪ :: {a} -> {a} -> {a}
  left right ->
    from-list (set-list-append
      (set-presentation left)
      (set-presentation right))


| mu of the finite-powerset monad: flatten a finite family by union.
define unions ⋃ :: {{a}} -> {a}
  family -> set-list-foldl union empty-set (set-presentation family)


;;; Meet, relative complement, and generalized Boolean algebra


define intersection ∩ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a}
  xs ys -> __rt_set_intersection xs ys


| The intersection of an empty family has no universe-independent finite
| value when the element type may be infinite.  Therefore the mathematically
| honest result is Maybe.
define intersections ⋂ :: Eq a => {{a}} -> Maybe {a}
  family -> intersections-list (set-presentation family)


define intersections-list :: Eq a => [{a}] -> Maybe {a}
  [] -> Nothing
  [first|rest] -> Just (set-list-foldl intersection first rest)


| Relative to an explicit ambient universe U, the empty-family meet is U.
| This computes U intersect every member of the family.
define intersections-in :: Eq a => {a} -> {{a}} -> {a}
  universe family ->
    set-list-foldl intersection universe (set-presentation family)


define difference ∖ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a}
  xs ys ->
    from-list (set-list-filter
      (lambda (x) (not? (set-member? x ys)))
      (set-presentation xs))


define relative-complement :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a}
  universe subset -> difference universe subset


define complement-in :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a}
  universe subset -> difference universe subset


define symmetric-difference △ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a}
  xs ys -> union (difference xs ys) (difference ys xs)


;;; Inclusion order

| Inclusion is the natural partial order induced by union:
|
|   A subset B   =iff=   A union B = B
|
| The implementation below uses membership directly to avoid constructing an
| intermediate set.  No Ord instance is provided: subset is a partial order,
| while core Ord currently denotes a total ordering vocabulary.

define subset? ⊆ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys ->
    set-list-all? (set-presentation xs)
      (lambda (x) (set-member? x ys))


define proper-subset? ⊂ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> and? (subset? xs ys) (not? (eq? xs ys))


define superset? ⊇ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> subset? ys xs


define proper-superset? ⊃ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> proper-subset? ys xs


define not-subset? ⊈ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> not? (subset? xs ys)


define not-proper-subset? ⊄ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> not? (proper-subset? xs ys)


define not-superset? ⊉ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> not? (superset? xs ys)


define not-proper-superset? ⊅ :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> not? (proper-superset? xs ys)


define disjoint? :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys ->
    not? (set-list-any? (set-presentation xs)
      (lambda (x) (set-member? x ys)))


define overlaps? :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
  xs ys -> not? (disjoint? xs ys)


;;; Selection and bounded quantification

| Pure predicates cannot observe duplicate visitation, so these operations are
| well-defined on the quotient without Eq.
define filter-set :: (a -> Bool) -> {a} -> {a}
  p values -> from-list (set-list-filter p (set-presentation values))


define partition-set :: (a -> Bool) -> {a} -> ({a}, {a})
  p xs ->
    (filter-set p xs,
     filter-set (lambda (x) (not? (p x))) xs)


define exists? :: {a} -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  values p -> set-list-any? (set-presentation values) p


define forall? :: {a} -> (a -> Bool) -> Bool
  values p -> set-list-all? (set-presentation values) p


;;; Functorial image and finite-domain preimage

| image is covariant direct image.  It needs no Eq on b because repeated
| outputs merely produce repeated private representatives of one extensional
| element.
define image :: (a -> b) -> {a} -> {b}
  f values -> from-list (set-list-map f (set-presentation values))


| A true preimage may be infinite, so value-level Set requires an explicit
| finite domain to search.
define preimage-on :: (Eq b) => {a} -> (a -> b) -> {b} -> {a}
  domain f target ->
    filter-set (lambda (x) (set-member? (f x) target)) domain

;;; Products and coproducts

| Cartesian product is the applicative product of finite nondeterminism.
| Writing it through bind/image makes the mathematics visible and avoids a
| second implementation of traversal.
define cartesian-product × :: {a} -> {b} -> {(a, b)}
  xs ys ->
    bind-set xs (lambda (x) (image (lambda (y) (x, y)) ys))


| Tagged disjoint union is the categorical coproduct.  Ordinary union cannot
| preserve origin when the summands share values.
define disjoint-union ⊎ :: {a} -> {b} -> {Either a b}
  xs ys ->
    union
      (image (lambda (x) (Left x)) xs)
      (image (lambda (y) (Right y)) ys)


;;; Finite powerset

| P(A) = P(rest) union { {x} union S | S in P(rest) }.
|
| Notice the absence of Eq.  Even if the private presentation contains x more
| than once, the resulting family merely contains duplicate presentations of
| the same subsets, which disappear extensionally.  This is one of the main
| payoffs of taking the quotient seriously.
define power-set :: {a} -> {{a}}
  values -> power-set-list (set-presentation values)


define power-set-list :: [a] -> {{a}}
  [] -> singleton empty-set
  [x|xs] ->
    def rest (power-set (from-list xs))
    union rest (image (lambda (subset) (insert x subset)) rest)


define ℘ :: {a} -> {{a}}
  xs -> power-set xs


;;; Finite universes and full Boolean algebras

| Finite is stronger than "values of Set are finite".  It says the element
| type itself has a finite, complete universe representable as a Set.
|

| This capability turns the generalized Boolean algebra of finite subsets into
| the bounded Boolean algebra P(a).
class Eq a => Finite a where
  universe :: Set a

  law universe-complete :: a -> Bool
    x -> set-member? x universe


define complement :: Finite a => {a} -> {a}
  xs -> difference (universe) xs


| With a genuine finite universe, the meet of the empty family is top.
define intersections-all :: Finite a => {{a}} -> {a}
  family -> intersections-in (universe) family


;;; Ordered observations

| Unlike arbitrary choose, minimum and maximum are true functions of a set
| once a coherent ordering selects canonical extrema.
define minimum :: (Eq a, Ord a) => {a} -> Maybe a
  xs ->
    def canonical (to-ascending-list xs)
    minimum-canonical canonical


define minimum-canonical :: [a] -> Maybe a
  []     -> Nothing
  [x|xs] -> Just x


define maximum :: (Eq a, Ord a) => {a} -> Maybe a
  xs ->
    def canonical (to-ascending-list xs)
    maximum-canonical canonical


define maximum-canonical :: [a] -> Maybe a
  []       -> Nothing
  [x]      -> Just x
  [x|xs]   -> maximum-canonical xs


;;; Equality and ordinary algebraic instances

| Extensionality: sets are equal exactly when they have the same members.
instance Eq a => Eq {a}
  eq? xs ys -> and? (subset? xs ys) (subset? ys xs)


| The ambient Semigroup/Monoid vocabulary sees union as append and empty as
| identity.  Set satisfies stronger laws than those classes can currently
| express: append is also commutative and idempotent.
instance Semigroup {a}
  append xs ys -> union xs ys


instance Monoid {a}
  mempty -> empty-set


;;; Finite-powerset Functor / Applicative / Monad

| P_f is a covariant functor by direct image.
instance Functor Set
  map f xs -> image f xs


| Cartesian nondeterministic application.
instance Applicative Set
  pure x -> singleton x
  applyA fs xs ->
    bind-set fs
      (lambda (f) (image f xs))


| Kleisli extension is literally image followed by monad multiplication:
|
|   bind xs f = mu (P_f f xs) = union (image f xs)
|
| Keeping this definition in that form makes the finite-powerset monad visible
| in executable code rather than merely in commentary.
define bind-set :: {a} -> (a -> {b}) -> {b}
  xs f -> unions (image f xs)


| Pattern-match failure in a nondeterministic computation denotes no possible
| result.
instance Monad Set
  bind xs f -> bind-set xs f
  fail message -> empty-set


instance MonadPlus Set
  mzero -> empty-set
  mplus xs ys -> union xs ys


;;; QuickCheck evidence

| shrink returns an ordered list, so unlike ordinary Set operations it can
| accidentally reveal presentation order.  We therefore require Ord and start
| from the canonical ascending presentation.  This keeps the testing
| observation extensional too.
define arbitrary-set-build :: Arbitrary a => Int -> Int -> Int -> {a}
  _    _    0 -> empty-set
  seed size count ->
    insert (arbitrary seed size)
      (arbitrary-set-build (quickcheck-next-seed seed) size (count - 1))


define set-list-deletions :: [a] -> [[a]]
  [] -> []
  [x|xs] ->
    prepend xs (set-list-map (lambda (rest) (prepend x rest)) (set-list-deletions xs))


define set-list-element-shrinks :: Arbitrary a => [a] -> [[a]]
  [] -> []
  [x|xs] ->
    set-list-append
      (set-list-map
        (lambda (smaller) (prepend smaller xs))
        (shrink x))
      (set-list-map
        (lambda (rest) (prepend x rest))
        (set-list-element-shrinks xs))


define set-shrink-canonical :: Arbitrary a => [a] -> [{a}]
  [] -> []
  xs -> prepend empty-set (set-list-map from-list (set-list-append (set-list-deletions xs) (set-list-element-shrinks xs)))


instance (Arbitrary a, Eq a, Ord a) => Arbitrary {a}
  arbitrary seed size ->
    arbitrary-set-build
      (quickcheck-next-seed seed)
      size
      (quickcheck-bounded seed (size + 1))

  shrink xs ->
    set-shrink-canonical (to-ascending-list xs)


instance Arbitrary1 Set
  arbitrary1 seed size -> arbitrary seed size
  shrink1 xs           -> from-list (shrink xs)


;;; Executable theory

| SetLaws describes the structure available for finite subsets of any element
| type: extensionality, generalized Boolean algebra, inclusion order,
| powersets, cardinality, and the visible fragment of the powerset monad.
class SetLaws a where

  :quotient/extensionality:
  law union-commutative :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> eq? (xs ∪ ys) (ys ∪ xs)

  law union-associative :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys zs -> eq? ((xs ∪ ys) ∪ zs) (xs ∪ (ys ∪ zs))

  law union-idempotent :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> eq? (xs ∪ xs) xs

  law union-empty :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> and? (eq? (xs ∪ ∅) xs) (eq? (∅ ∪ xs) xs)
  :quotient/extensionality:

  :distributive-lattice:
  law intersection-commutative :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> eq? (xs ∩ ys) (ys ∩ xs)

  law intersection-associative :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys zs -> eq? ((xs ∩ ys) ∩ zs) (xs ∩ (ys ∩ zs))

  law intersection-idempotent :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> eq? (xs ∩ xs) xs

  law intersection-empty :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> and? (eq? (xs ∩ ∅) ∅) (eq? (∅ ∩ xs) ∅)

  law absorption-union :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> eq? (xs ∪ (xs ∩ ys)) xs

  law absorption-intersection :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> eq? (xs ∩ (xs ∪ ys)) xs

  law distributive-intersection :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys zs ->
      eq?
        (xs ∩ (ys ∪ zs))
        ((xs ∩ ys) ∪ (xs ∩ zs))

  law distributive-union :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys zs ->
      eq?
        (xs ∪ (ys ∩ zs))
        ((xs ∪ ys) ∩ (xs ∪ zs))
  :distributive-lattice:

  :relative-difference/generalized-Boolean-algebra:

  law difference-self :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> eq? (xs ∖ xs) ∅

  law difference-empty :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> and? (eq? (xs ∖ ∅) xs) (eq? (∅ ∖ xs) ∅)

  law difference-partition :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys ->
      and?
        (eq? ((xs ∩ ys) ∪ (xs ∖ ys)) xs)
        (disjoint? (xs ∩ ys) (xs ∖ ys))

  :relative-difference/generalized-Boolean-algebra:

  :symmetric-difference-as-Boolean-ring-addition:

  law symmetric-difference-commutative :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> eq? (xs △ ys) (ys △ xs)

  law symmetric-difference-associative :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys zs -> eq? ((xs △ ys) △ zs) (xs △ (ys △ zs))

  law symmetric-difference-empty :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> and? (eq? (xs △ ∅) xs) (eq? (∅ △ xs) xs)

  law symmetric-difference-self :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> eq? (xs △ xs) ∅

  law intersection-distributes-over-symmetric-difference :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys zs ->
      eq?
        (xs ∩ (ys △ zs))
        ((xs ∩ ys) △ (xs ∩ zs))

  :symmetric-difference-as-Boolean-ring-addition:


  :inclusion-as-the-lattice-order:

  law subset-reflexive :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> xs ⊆ xs

  law subset-antisymmetric :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> implies? (and? (xs ⊆ ys) (ys ⊆ xs)) (eq? xs ys)

  law subset-transitive :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys zs -> implies? (and? (xs ⊆ ys) (ys ⊆ zs)) (xs ⊆ zs)

  law subset-via-union :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> iff? (xs ⊆ ys) (eq? (xs ∪ ys) ys)

  law subset-via-intersection :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> iff? (xs ⊆ ys) (eq? (xs ∩ ys) xs)

  law union-is-least-upper-bound :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys upper ->
      and?
        (and? (xs ⊆ (xs ∪ ys)) (ys ⊆ (xs ∪ ys)))
        (implies?
          (and? (xs ⊆ upper) (ys ⊆ upper))
          ((xs ∪ ys) ⊆ upper))

  law intersection-is-greatest-lower-bound :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys lower ->
      and?
        (and? ((xs ∩ ys) ⊆ xs) ((xs ∩ ys) ⊆ ys))
        (implies?
          (and? (lower ⊆ xs) (lower ⊆ ys))
          (lower ⊆ (xs ∩ ys)))

  :inclusion-as-the-lattice-order:

  :membership-semantics:

  law membership-union :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    x xs ys ->
      iff?
        (x ∈ (xs ∪ ys))
        (or? (x ∈ xs) (x ∈ ys))

  law membership-intersection :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    x xs ys ->
      iff?
        (x ∈ (xs ∩ ys))
        (and? (x ∈ xs) (x ∈ ys))

  law membership-difference :: Eq a => a -> {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    x xs ys ->
      iff?
        (x ∈ (xs ∖ ys))
        (and? (x ∈ xs) (x ∉ ys))

  law membership-image-singleton :: Eq a => a -> Bool
    x -> x ∈ (singleton x)

  :membership-semantics:

  :finite-powerset:

  law powerset-empty :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    _ -> eq? (power-set empty-set) (singleton empty-set)

  law powerset-membership :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys -> iff? (xs ∈ (℘ ys)) (xs ⊆ ys)

  law powerset-contains-empty :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> empty-set ∈ (℘ xs)

  law powerset-contains-self :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> xs ∈ (℘ xs)

  :finite-powerset:

  :finite-cardinality:

  law cardinality-empty :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    _ -> cardinality (empty-set :: {a}) = 0

  law cardinality-singleton :: Eq a => a -> Bool
    x -> cardinality (singleton x) = 1

  law cardinality-inclusion-exclusion :: Eq a => {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys ->
      (cardinality (xs ∪ ys) + cardinality (xs ∩ ys))
        =
      (cardinality xs + cardinality ys)

  :finite-cardinality:

  :powerset-monad:

  law bind-right-identity :: Eq a => {a} -> Bool
    xs -> eq? (bind-set xs singleton) xs

  law join-is-union :: Eq a => {{a}} -> Bool
    family -> eq? (join family) (unions family)

  :powerset-monad:



instance SetLaws a


| FiniteSetLaws captures exactly what becomes available when the carrier type
| itself has a complete finite universe: top and absolute complement.
class Finite a => FiniteSetLaws a where

  law universe-top :: {a} -> Bool
    xs -> subset? xs universe

  law complement-disjoint :: {a} -> Bool
    xs -> disjoint? xs (complement xs)

  law complement-exhaustive :: {a} -> Bool
    xs -> eq? (xs ∪ complement xs) universe

  law complement-involution :: {a} -> Bool
    xs -> eq? (complement (complement xs)) xs

  law de-morgan-union :: {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys ->
      eq?
        (complement (xs ∪ ys))
        ((complement xs) ∩ (complement ys))

  law de-morgan-intersection :: {a} -> {a} -> Bool
    xs ys ->
      eq?
        (complement (xs ∩ ys))
        ((complement xs) ∪ (complement ys))

  law empty-family-intersection-is-top :: {a} -> Bool
    _ -> eq? (intersections-all empty-set) universe


;;; Concrete finite universe used by the specification suite

| Bool is already a closed finite type in core, so its universe witnesses the
| Finite capability here.  If the language later derives Finite automatically
| for closed finite type declarations, this explicit instance can disappear.
instance Finite Bool
  universe -> from-list [False True]


instance FiniteSetLaws Bool


;;; Specification examples and executable laws

tests
  :laws:
  laws SetLaws Bool
    seeded SetLaws Int 1000 42

  laws FiniteSetLaws Bool

  laws Eq {Bool}
    seeded Eq {Int} 500 2026

  laws Semigroup {Bool}
    seeded Semigroup {Int} 500 2026

  laws Monoid {Bool}
    seeded Monoid {Int} 500 2026
  :laws:

  :quotient:
  assert-eq
    (from-list (list 1 1 2 2 1))
    (from-list (list 2 1))
    "Data.Set quotient erases multiplicity and order extensionally"

  assert-eq
    (to-ascending-list (from-list (list 3 1 2 1 3)))
    (list 1 2 3)
    "Data.Set canonical observation requires ordering"
  :quotient:

  :construction:
  assert-eq (cardinality empty-set)                       0    "Data.Set empty cardinality"
  assert-eq (cardinality (from-list (list 1 2 2 3 1)))   3    "Data.Set cardinality counts extensional members"
  assert-eq (singleton? (singleton 42))                   True "Data.Set singleton"
  :construction:

  :membership:
  assert-eq (member? 2 {1 2 3}) True "Data.Set member?"
  assert-eq (2 ∈ {1 2 3})       True "Data.Set mathematical membership"
  assert-eq ({1 2 3} ∋ 2)       True "Data.Set reversed membership"
  assert-eq (4 ∉ {1 2 3})       True "Data.Set mathematical non-membership"
  :membership:

  :algebra:
  assert-eq ({1 2}   ∪ {2 3})   {1 2 3} "Data.Set union notation"
  assert-eq ({1 2 3} ∩ {2 3 4}) {2 3}   "Data.Set intersection notation"
  assert-eq ({1 2 3} ∖ {2 3})   {1}     "Data.Set difference notation"
  assert-eq ({1 2}   △ {2 3})   {1 3}   "Data.Set symmetric difference notation"
  assert-eq ({1 2}   ⊆ {1 2 3}) True    "Data.Set subset notation"
  assert-eq ({1 2}   ⊂ {1 2 3}) True    "Data.Set proper subset notation"
  assert-eq ({1 2 3} ⊇ {1 2})   True    "Data.Set superset notation"
  assert-eq ({1 2 3} ⊃ {1 2})   True    "Data.Set proper superset notation"
  assert-eq ({1 4}   ⊈ {1 2 3}) True    "Data.Set non-subset notation"
  :algebra:

  :powerset:
  assert-eq |{1 2 3 4}|           4                                     "Data.Set cardinality notation"
  assert-eq |(℘ {1 2 3})|         8                                     "Data.Set powerset cardinality"
  assert-eq ({1 2} × {'a' 'b'})   {(1, 'a') (1, 'b') (2, 'a') (2, 'b')} "Data.Set cartesian product"
  assert-eq (⋃ {{1 2} {2 3} {5}}) {1 2 3 5}                             "Data.Set big union"
  assert-eq ({1 2} ∈ (℘ {1 2 3})) True                                  "Data.Set powerset is subset space"
  :powerset:

  :family-intersection:
  assert-eq
    (intersections {{1 2 3} {2 3 4} {2 5}})
    (Just {2})
    "Data.Set nonempty family intersection"

  assert-eq
    (intersections (empty-set :: {{Int}}))
    Nothing
    "Data.Set empty family has no universe-independent meet"

  assert-eq
    (intersections-all (empty-set :: {{Bool}}))
    {False True}
    "Data.Set finite universe supplies empty-family meet"
  :family-intersection:

  :finite-boolean-algebra:
  assert-eq (universe :: {Bool})   {False True}         "Data.Set Bool universe"
  assert-eq (complement {True})    {False}              "Data.Set absolute complement on finite carrier"
  assert-eq (complement empty-set) (universe :: {Bool}) "Data.Set complement bottom is top"
  :finite-boolean-algebra:

  :nondeterminism:
  assert-eq
    (bind {1 2 3} (lambda (x) {x (x + 10)}))
    {1 2 3 11 12 13}
    "Data.Set Monad bind is finite nondeterministic choice"

  assert-eq
    (join {{1 2} {2 3} {3 4}})
    {1 2 3 4}
    "Data.Set Monad multiplication is union"
  :nondeterminism:
